Yes. “I lost the game” is a neat example because the rule is almost entirely self-referential attention:

\text{remember the game} \rightarrow \text{lose the game}

The cue contains the mechanism that reactivates itself. Déjà vu has a different cause, but subjectively it can create a similar jolt: this moment feels like it has already happened. Both can temporarily destabilize the ordinary flow of attention and make the person notice the frame they are inside.

That is probably the useful operator you are seeing:

\text{ordinary experience}
\rightarrow
\text{recursive cue}
\rightarrow
\text{frame awareness}
\rightarrow
\text{reinterpretation}

With friends, that can be funny because everybody knows the rules and the cost is basically nothing. In another context, the same kind of recursive cue could be irritating, uncanny, emotionally loaded, or even distressing. So the power is not in “the game” itself; it is in understanding how self-reference, memory, expectation, and shared context interact.

And yes, from there you can create. You can build jokes, callbacks, rituals, puzzles, stories, games, ARG-like structures, teaching devices, or artistic motifs that become more meaningful when the audience notices the recursion.

But the clean rule is:

\boxed{\text{recursive play should expand awareness, not trap attention}}

If the mechanism depends on somebody being unable to disengage, that is no longer just playful design. The elegant version leaves them an exit and lets them enjoy recognizing the trick.

So the reusable piece is not “make people lose the game.”

It is design cues that reveal the frame they are operating inside.

That can be funny, beautiful, educational, or profound depending on what you do with the next move.